Alright, here's my vision.  Imagine a roguelikes portal, but it
wouldn't be an all-encompassing portal in the traditional sense.  It'd
basically contain two main parts: a "blogroll" and a blog.  The
blogroll wouldn't really be a blogroll, but rather, a collection of
useful links to other Roguelikes sites on the web, including
roguebasin (which I wasn't previously aware of) and everything else.
The blog would be written by multiple authors and would contain
articles on anything related to roguelikes: announcements of new
releases, discussion of "modern" roguelike features, etc.  I'd give
out posting access very liberally, so anyone who has something good to
say would be able to do it.

Alright, so it's not incredibly grandiose.  But it's easily doable and
could become a very useful resource.  I loved the roguelike netzine,
but it looks like they only got out two issues.  That's very
unfortunate.  I just don't think a netzine format makes as much sense
as, say, a blog.  There's no need to arbitrarily hold up a collection
of articles until enough are written to create an "issue".  I think a
blog that simply published articles as they were written would be
useful.

I'm willing to be the editor (I have some college-level newspaper
experience, nothing tremendously ground-breaking, but experience
nonetheless).  And I'd like to write some articles over time about
Nethack, which is sort of my tried-and-true roguelike.  I think the
hardest part would be getting others who are interested in writing
about roguelike stuff.  Hence this post, trying to gauge interest.



